  "experiment": {
    "goal": "Check on static samples whether ethanol in beer fermentation can be quantified with a fiber-optic Raman probe, and compare with MIR ATR as a check of the customer's premise (fiber length and aqueous medium), before any in-line campaign.",
    "sample_count": "ca. 100 samples: ca. 40 designed spiked samples, 2 calibration brews with ca. 15 time points each, and 2 independent validation brews with ca. 15 time points each.",
    "sample_design": "Ethanol and sugar change in opposite directions during fermentation, so real brew samples alone give a correlated calibration. Add designed samples: beer base and wort spiked with ethanol and sugar in a crossed design, so that ethanol varies independently of residual sugar. Cover an ethanol range from zero to above the expected final value (range to be confirmed by the customer). Vary temperature over the expected fermentation range, yeast and turbidity level, dissolved CO2, and recipe. Add brew samples over the whole fermentation time as realistic calibration and validation batches.",
    "interferents": [
      "Residual sugar and other extract components (correlate with ethanol)",
      "Yeast cells and turbidity",
      "CO2 bubbles",
      "Temperature",
      "Colour and fluorescence of the beer matrix (Raman)",
      "Differences between recipes and brews"
    ],
    "reference_method": "No reference method exists yet. Options for the customer: distillation with density measurement, GC-FID, or HPLC with refractive-index detection. Agree the method, its uncertainty and the sampling time before starting.",
    "methods_compared": [
      "raman",
      "mir"
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Step 1: Check on representative samples (several recipes and fermentation stages) whether fluorescence background swamps the Raman signal, with 785 nm and, if available, 1064 nm excitation.",
      "Step 2: Agree the reference method and its uncertainty, then take the reference values for all samples.",
      "Step 3: Measure the designed spiked samples with a Raman immersion probe or non-contact optics on a silica fiber, and in parallel with MIR ATR.",
      "Step 4: Check of the customer's premise: measure the same samples with MIR ATR once with a short fiber and once with the 50 m fiber from the brochure (if the customer owns one), and compare signal level and noise.",
      "Step 5: Build calibrations on at least two calibration batches (designed samples plus 2 calibration brews), per method.",
      "Step 6: Validate on independent brews that were not used for calibration. Report bias, RMSEP and any dependence on turbidity, temperature and CO2.",
      "Step 7: Only after a passed lab study, plan an in-line trial at the fermenter (measurement point, sampling port, cleaning) and the analyzer location."
    ],
    "success_criterion": "On the independent validation brews, bias against the chosen reference at most 0.1 % absolute ethanol and RMSEP at most 0.2 % absolute over the full range, with no systematic dependence on temperature or turbidity. These limits are a proposal and are replaced by the customer's accuracy requirement once it is known.",
    "duration_estimate": "ca. 6–8 weeks, driven by the fermentation time of the calibration and validation brews. An in-line campaign comes afterwards."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "text": "The MIR ATR concept with the analyzer in a control room in another building cannot be approved on the brochure claim. Decisive facts are missing: the real cable route, the fiber type, and the ethanol range. Owning an analyzer or cable, or a 50 m figure from a flyer, is not evidence that it works for this process. What would still meet the goal: place the MIR analyzer next to the fermenter and send the ethanol result digitally to the control room, or use a method with long silica fibers such as Raman.",
      "severity": "high"
    },
    {
      "text": "Ranking vs assessment: The ranking blocks MIR because MIR fibers are only a few meters long, while the assessment keeps MIR ATR as needs_info with no AFS support and does not exclude it. The fiber-length block rests on a draft card and the customer's 50 m claim is a business source, so neither side is proven. This needs a datasheet check. The ranking also drops NIR with a water penalty, while the assessment lists NIR as a candidate (no evidence). If ethanol is at percent level, which is not yet confirmed, that water penalty is likely too harsh. Raman is the only method with an AFS application (S01 p.16), and the ranking and assessment agree on it. That is the better supported side.",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "text": "No reference values exist yet. A quantitative result needs an independent reference to build and test a calibration, so the status stays provisional until a reference method and samples are available.",
      "severity": "high"
    },
    {
      "text": "Brochure and flyer figures are business context, not evidence of technical performance. They should not enter the recommendation without a datasheet.",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "text": "Raman: fluorescence background from the beer matrix can swamp the signal. Needs the lab check before anything else. The Raman signal is also weak, and the ethanol range is unknown.",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "text": "Fermentation specifics: CO2 bubbles, yeast and turbidity can disturb the optical signal and foul the window. Ethanol and sugar are correlated in a real brew, so a calibration on brew samples alone can look good and still fail.",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "text": "Water bands: in MIR and NIR strong water absorption limits usable regions and sensitivity in an aqueous medium. Whether this matters depends on the ethanol range, which is not yet known.",
      "severity": "low"
    }
  ],
